Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Greetings.  I've been given a document format that I'm required to follow, and it uses Roman numerals for section numbers.  I've got an Rmd document that I "typeset" with the render command in the rmarkdown package.  It works nicely, but it has Arabic numerals for the sections.  Is there any way to get Roman numerals?  In LaTeX it would be something like:

\documentclass{article}
\renewcommand{\thesection}{\Roman{section}}
\renewcommand{\thesubsection}{\thesection.\Roman{subsection}}

Here's the info about the rmarkdown that I'm using:

Package:              rmarkdown
Type:                 Package
Title:                R Markdown Document Conversion
Version:              0.1.6
Date:                 2014-01-26

And the version of R:

> sessionInfo()
R version 3.1.0 (2014-04-10)
Platform: x86_64-redhat-linux-gnu (64-bit)

Thanks.

-- Mike

From uc2a4124666613378 at groups.google.com  Fri May 23 13:29:40 2014
From: uc2a4124666613378 at groups.google.com (Keith Hughitt)
Date: Fri, 23 May 2014 13:29:40 +0000
Subject: Reusing figures for multiple output formats?
Message-ID: <nBuXV8Y_1R4.R1JCbPezXZEJ@knitr.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/knitr/c/nBuXV8Y_1R4/m/R1JCbPezXZEJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello,

Does anyone happen to know of a way to generate a figure once, and reuse the output for multiple output formats?

By using the caching feature of knitr, it's possible to avoid rerunning computationally-intensive bits of code when outputting to multiple formats, however, plots are typically still created anew for each additional format, which can take a long time.

The main two formats I'm interested in outputting to at the moment are HTML and PDF and I am using rmarkdown to handle the conversion. I've tried setting dev='png' to force the PDF output to use PNG, but it ends up generating a second version of the PNG in a different sub-directory (figure-html and figure-latex). I also tried setting dev to both "pdf" and "png", but this also does not solve the issue.

Here is an example .Rmd file to demonstrate the issue:

> ---
> output: [html_document, pdf_document]
> ---
>
> Test
> ====
>
> To reproduce:
>
> > library(rmarkdown)
> > render('knitr_multiple_formats.rmd', output_format='all')
>
> ```{r knitr_settings, include=FALSE}
> library(knitr)
> opts_chunk$set(dev='png')
> ```
>
> ```{r block1, cache=TRUE}
> x = matrix(rnorm(1500^2), ncol=1500)
> hist(x)
> ```
>
> ```{r block2, cache=TRUE}
> library(gplots)
>
> t1 = Sys.time()
> heatmap.2(x, trace='none')
> t2 = Sys.time()
>
> print(t2 - t1)
> ```

When the above example, it only takes a minute or so to generate each copy of the plot, however, you as the size of the data matrix grows, the added burden of regenerating the plot becomes significant.

Anyone know of a way around this?

Any suggestions would be greatly appreciated.

Cheers,
Keith

