It is not an easy problem. I just sent a PR to rmarkdown:
https://github.com/rstudio/rmarkdown/pull/108 It is not an optimal
solution, but it will assign each output format a separate cache
directory, so that the cache is not invalidated when you switch
between output formats. Note, however, the code chunk has to be
executed for each output format for at least once.

Regards,
Yihui
--
Yihui Xie <xiey...@gmail.com>
Web: http://yihui.name

On Fri, May 23, 2014 at 8:29 AM, Keith Hughitt <keith....@gmail.com> wrote:
> Hello,
>
> Does anyone happen to know of a way to generate a figure once, and reuse the
> output for multiple output formats?
>
> By using the caching feature of knitr, it's possible to avoid rerunning
> computationally-intensive bits of code when outputting to multiple formats,
> however, plots are typically still created anew for each additional format,
> which can take a long time.
>
> The main two formats I'm interested in outputting to at the moment are HTML
> and PDF and I am using rmarkdown to handle the conversion. I've tried
> setting dev='png' to force the PDF output to use PNG, but it ends up
> generating a second version of the PNG in a different sub-directory
> (figure-html and figure-latex). I also tried setting dev to both "pdf" and
> "png", but this also does not solve the issue.
>
> Here is an example .Rmd file to demonstrate the issue:
>
> ---
> output: [html_document, pdf_document]
> ---
>
> Test
> ====
>
> To reproduce:
>
>> library(rmarkdown)
>> render('knitr_multiple_formats.rmd', output_format='all')
>
> ```{r knitr_settings, include=FALSE}
> library(knitr)
> opts_chunk$set(dev='png')
> ```
>
> ```{r block1, cache=TRUE}
> x = matrix(rnorm(1500^2), ncol=1500)
> hist(x)
> ```
>
> ```{r block2, cache=TRUE}
> library(gplots)
>
> t1 = Sys.time()
> heatmap.2(x, trace='none')
> t2 = Sys.time()
>
> print(t2 - t1)
> ```
>
>
> When the above example, it only takes a minute or so to generate each copy
> of the plot, however, you as the size of the data matrix grows, the added
